-- Passage rg5-evidence-inference-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('058177d1-2948-5dcf-a08d-1c9cad5ece08', 'rg5-evidence-inference-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-evidence-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Libraries on Wheels$q$, $q$More than a hundred years ago, many families lived far from any town. Children on farms might never visit a library. A few librarians decided that if people could not come to the books, the books would go to the people.

The earliest traveling libraries were simple. A librarian loaded wooden boxes of books onto a horse-drawn wagon. The wagon stopped at farms, general stores and country schools. Families chose a few books and kept them until the wagon came back, often a month later.

Later, libraries used trucks with shelves built inside. These were called bookmobiles. A bookmobile could carry far more books than a wagon. It could also travel greater distances in one day. Some had small desks and stools, so readers could step inside and browse as if they were in a tiny library.

Bookmobile drivers learned what their regular readers liked. A driver might save a new adventure book for a boy who had finished every other one on the shelves. Some readers waited at the end of a dirt road for an hour before the bookmobile arrived.

Bookmobiles still travel today. Some visit neighborhoods without a nearby library branch. Others stop at senior centers or summer camps. Many now carry tablets and offer internet access along with printed books.$q$, 'draft')
